runtime changes: 0.4.33 → 0.4.35
## 0.4.35

- Column privacy: append `read <- policy` to a column or share `read (a, b) <- policy`. Effective authority intersects row, column and owner-private rules for queries, write results and downloads. Whole rows omit hidden properties; explicit selections return null and generated read properties are optional. Row-local cells sync; witness-dependent cells stay server-side with automatic online fallback. Protected payloads cannot supply structural keys, authority, lifetimes or maintained inputs (E_COLUMN_READ). Existing fields keep their audience until a rule is declared; old clients receive compatibility advice for newly optional results.
- Release evolution: explicit `down` projections preserve representable old views, and identical-type scalar columns support `renamed from`. Native and old-view cells must be authoritative scalars; reference inputs keep their lifetime bindings. Forward restores exact retained cells and catches up declared backfills for old-release writes; conflicting edits refuse. Resolving dormant rows, retired columns and sparse defaults preserves receipt-owned physical absence. Read-through predicate changes preserve captured authority/lifetime. Rollback previews lead with hidden old-table records, then empty/defaulted legacy cells and newer-only rows; deploy previews combine visibility risks, old-client contracts and the security campaign summary. Counts describe schema populations before read rules. Unrepresentable values stay dormant, separately from deletion.
- Inspection: `sql --dormant --release DIR` shows retained rows/cells, including removed tables; `sql --all-releases --release DIR` adds active images. State/source/provided-column labels identify retained images, not historical snapshots. `data --json` now returns `{rows, more, limit}`; scripts expecting a bare array must read `rows`. Its default limit remains 50.
- Input compatibility: `explanation: text 1..500 alias reason` accepts and validates the old spelling without dropping its value. Supplying both spellings refuses E_INPUT. Generated callers, availability checks and old-client reports understand aliases. Preserve wrappers when result shapes also change.
- Invariant quality: state independent actor/action/column intent from the product spec. N_INVARIANT_RESTATES_RULE advises on equivalent rule copies; generated drafts remain unaccepted questions. Column update intent covers actual column edits; write intent also covers inserts. Default test generates isolated per-role fixtures, ownership, relationships and verified media, including empty fixture directories and password-only apps. Authored seed/fixture counterexamples fail again. Generated creators preserve guards and copied values; initial worlds remain explicit test assumptions. Committed role/relationship histories retain their actual steps. Ephemeral mutation histories remain uncovered while durable campaigns work beside ephemeral declarations.
- Check summarizes proved/refuted/unproved intent, exposure and ranked notes; escalation locations stay visible. Matching test refutations persist only for identical source, fixtures and CLI bytes. Bounded check exhaustion stays unproved. Rule-changing `deploy --check` asks for a current finite test campaign and reports exposure without adding an activation veto. Start teaches check → test → audit; finite campaigns are not universal security proofs.
- Staging and upgrades: start, cheatsheet and personas point shared previews and real-user proofs to staging. seedAccounts accepts a service seat and merges custom arguments; first-admin provisioning replays on persistent stores. Upgrade prints project-specific closed-door and persistent startDev warnings, including quiet/JSON callers. Memory token discovery, clock/reset session advice and the uploads alias/ownership recipes improve recovery. Existing custom scripts still need review.
- Identity and lifecycle: SDK guest/signup/login, seedAccounts and proof seedUsers honor explicit identity retryAfterMs with bounded retry/cancellation; ambiguous requests never replay. Deploy/rollback wait briefly for a shutting-down store owner; startServe().stop() waits for retained children and worker-held locks. `serve DIR --wait-stopped` provides a bounded read-only lock barrier.
- Read semantics: mutually exclusive returning query branches share their maximum physical reservation, fixing empty partial results; sequential and loop work remain bounded. N_QUERY_FALLTHROUGH, N_QUERY_RESERVATION and N_LOOKUP_FILTERED explain incomplete paths and private optional lookups. Optional points return null for hidden/missing/expired rows; asserted points keep their authored refusal and precedence.
- Existing apps/stores retain rows, ids, users, dormant values and rollback history. Upgrading recognizes retained source across compiler versions and fixes read-through preview/restoration bookkeeping. Opening migration evidence upgrades it transactionally to release-store format 7; use the newer CLI for subsequent source rollback/forward, because older binaries refuse that format. Regenerate declarations and rebuild web/native/Exact engines for new column/alias capabilities. Upgrade alone invents no column policy, reverse mapping, backfill or side-table reconciliation.

## 0.4.34

- Deploy reactivates matching retained source under its existing release id after rollback, validates privately and removes definitively refused candidates. Deploy and rollback share `--restore-dormant`; previews report current and future rollback hazards with bridge commands. Old-client reports compare operation inputs, results and refusals. Literal query/mutation defaults, validated ignored deprecated arguments and same-module aliases preserve existing callers; required inputs remain required in generated types.
- Declare typed historical backfills in Quarry for conditional values, derived fields, text splits and enum renames. `deploy --check` previews actual transformed counts alongside the old-client diff. Activation commits data, indexes and release together; rollback restores eligible preimages and refuses conflicting edits. Reactivation catches up old-release edits while preserving authored outputs. The packed-tested `guide deploy` recipe exercises real users and one persistent production store across v1 → v2 → rollback → forward.
- Staging stores accept `serve --clock-offset` and full-service clock status/advance. `startServe` exposes `clockOffset`, `now()` and `advance()`; production refuses every clock control and the other dev doors stay closed.
- Column read invariants (`invariant table.column read only P`) and per-column audit audiences extend declared security intent. Generated histories fork complete fixture stores without re-seeding maintained fields, exercise principals/references/enums/media/time and empty-start writes, and replay media before role revocation. Only fixture-reachable policy violations fail acceptance; `check --all`, `audit --all` and `test --all` expose supplemental seed/synthetic observations. Finite challenges retain their stated proof limits.
- Located security notes reduce unsupported existence/grant warnings and add structural privilege-escalation advice with the first-admin recipe. Rule printing preserves parentheses; identity rate refusals include retry delays. Audit includes aliases with their shared guards.
- Existing 0.4.33 apps and stores retain rows, ids, users and rollback history. Opening a store upgrades release/backfill metadata. Use 0.4.34 for subsequent source rollback; older binaries refuse the upgraded release-store format. Keep a backup if you need to downgrade the CLI. Upgrading alone adds no invariants, backfills, aliases or authority. Regenerate declarations and rebuild web/native/Exact engines; review the new located notes and declare intended column policy. Custom proofs and application screens remain owned by the app.
History begins at 0.4.26; earlier changes are not recorded.
